          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "The heavy squeezing feeling you describe behind your breastbone when walking uphill, which eases with rest, could be a sign of angina. Guidance on chest discomfort specifies that any pressure, tightness, or squeezing in the chest lasting more than a few minutes, especially accompanied by other symptoms, should be treated as a possible heart attack until assessed by a healthcare professional. It's essential to seek immediate medical help if you experience such symptoms (source: Emergency medicine and cardiology society guidance).\n\nIf it turns out you have angina, the guidance indicates that physical activity is recommended. Adults are generally advised to aim for 150 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic activity weekly, along with muscle-strengthening activities. Moderate-intensity means you should be breathing harder and able to talk but not sing (source: General clinical reference and physical activity guidance). \n\nI cannot provide specific advice on medication management or personalized treatment plans, nor can I assess your individual condition. Please consult with a healthcare professional for tailored guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
